# IAM Comparison: AWS vs Azure

## 1) Core identity object
- AWS: IAM User (long-term identity in AWS account)
- Azure: User in Entra ID (Azure AD)

Practical scenario:
A new hire joins a company.
- AWS: create IAM user (or use federation/SSO) and attach permissions
- Azure: create Entra user and assign roles/groups

## 2) Groups
- AWS: IAM Groups (collection of users; permissions via policies)
- Azure: Entra ID Groups (used for app access + RBAC assignments)

Scenario:
“Warehouse supervisors” group should access inventory dashboards.
- AWS: group policy grants read access to dashboards/resources
- Azure: group assigned RBAC role on resource scope

## 3) Roles (important difference!)
- AWS: IAM Role = assumed identity (temporary credentials) via STS
- Azure: RBAC Role = set of permissions; the *identity* is user/service principal/managed identity

Scenario:
A CI/CD pipeline deploys to cloud.
- AWS: pipeline assumes a role (temporary tokens) to deploy
- Azure: pipeline uses a service principal or managed identity; granted RBAC role on subscription/resource group

## 4) Policies / Permissions definition
- AWS: IAM Policy JSON (Allow/Deny, Actions, Resources, Conditions)
- Azure: Role Definition (built-in/custom roles), permissions like Actions/NotActions

Key idea:
- AWS IAM is “policy-first”
- Azure RBAC is “role assignment-first”

## 5) Permission attachment / assignment
- AWS: attach policy to user/group/role
- Azure: assign RBAC role to principal at a scope

## 6) Scope model
- AWS: policies reference ARNs; scope is by resource and conditions
- Azure: scope is explicit hierarchy:
  Management Group → Subscription → Resource Group → Resource

Scenario:
Give Dev team access only to “dev” environment.
- AWS: restrict to specific ARNs (dev resources)
- Azure: assign role only at dev resource group scope

## 7) Service-to-service identity
- AWS: IAM Role for service + Instance Profile / task role (EC2/ECS/EKS)
- Azure: Managed Identity (system/user assigned)

Scenario:
VM needs to read secrets securely.
- AWS: EC2 role + policy to Secrets Manager/SSM
- Azure: Managed identity + role assignment to Key Vault

## 8) Temporary credentials
- AWS: STS assumed roles are default for modern workloads
- Azure: access tokens via Entra ID; managed identities rotate automatically

## 9) Auditing / logs
- AWS: CloudTrail (who did what), IAM Access Analyzer
- Azure: Activity Log, Entra sign-in logs, PIM auditing

## Quick mapping table
AWS IAM User = Entra ID User  
AWS IAM Group = Entra ID Group  
AWS IAM Role = (Azure identity) Service Principal / Managed Identity (then RBAC role assigned)  
AWS IAM Policy = Azure RBAC Role Definition  
Policy attachment = Role Assignment  
ARN resource scope = Azure Scope hierarchy